PACK link 1
IMPORT boot 1
RESIDENT Vm
VIRTUAL Page
RESIDENT LinkWrite
RESIDENT Peers
-- THE GRAPH LINK, SLICE 1: WHAT THE PAGE TELLS THE SEAT (#1143, docs/graph-link.md sections 2 and 8, "Slice 1: built"). The page is a second field engine; it cannot be asked, so it tells.
-- Its own pack (apps/barest.c1f/link_view.c1f, run in the browser) writes through the ordinary door (`POST /mcp`, `entity_write`; whoever reaches the page may write, there are no per-person rights):
--   Vm:<ns>.caps              what this page can do, ONE write at start: protocol, encodings, frames, the DOM allow-list, the packs it runs (`provides`, each with its `requires`), the classes it can evaluate, limits, device class
--   <ns>::Page:main.report    what it loaded and how it is: packs (name: size), load_errors, view, hidden, width class, build, host version; written again whenever one of those changes (never on a timer)
-- `<ns>` is `p` + 8 hex digits, made by the SEAT for each load of the page (door_fieldvm.c1f, `Request.fv_page`) and given to the page in the snapshot as `Link:page.ns`: a page cannot name itself.
-- The class `Vm` is the page's ROOT (one stored row per page; kind: the page). `Page` is VIRTUAL (a report is not memory: a restart forgets it) and is HELD by its `Vm` through `Vm.page`, a reference, so it is
-- not collected. A restart retires every `Vm` (the tabs of the last run do not report again until they are loaded again).
-- THE SEAT STAMPS THE TIME (the page has no wall clock it is held to): `Vm.seen` is `clock` at the last write of `caps` or `report`; `Vm.connected_at` at the first. `Vm.state` is below: slice 2 replaced the slice-1 guess
-- (`stale` after `link_stale_s` = 120 s, no heartbeat) with the LEASE of a page that talks (its link frames are its heartbeat).

Page.ns getter: before(this.eid, '::')
-- THE LINK'S NUMBERS (docs/graph-link.md "Slice 2: built"; each one is the operator's pick of that list, a fact may override it): the seat reads them from `Boot`, so a pack and a test read one place.
Boot.lk_hold getter: num(this.link_hold_s) if this.link_hold_s != None else 25
Boot.lk_lease getter: num(this.link_lease_s) if this.link_lease_s != None else 60
Boot.lk_lease_hidden getter: num(this.link_lease_hidden_s) if this.link_lease_hidden_s != None else 600
Boot.lk_window getter: num(this.link_window) if this.link_window != None else 8
Boot.lk_max_subs getter: num(this.link_max_subs) if this.link_max_subs != None else 200
Boot.lk_max_links getter: num(this.link_max_links) if this.link_max_links != None else 16
Boot.lk_max_value getter: num(this.link_max_value) if this.link_max_value != None else 65536
Boot.lk_max_frame getter: num(this.link_max_frame) if this.link_max_frame != None else 262144
-- the boot epoch: a value the seat's NEXT run cannot repeat (a page that sees it change resubscribes everything)
Boot.started on-setter link-epoch:
  ALWAYS:
    this.lk_since = clock
Boot.lk_epoch getter: str(this.lk_since) if this.lk_since != None else None
-- A PAGE THAT TALKS IS CONNECTED (slice 2; this replaces the slice-1 guess of 120 s of silence): every frame of the link (packs/link_route.c1f) is a word from the page, kept on the page's `LinkPeer` (RAM: a frame is not history),
-- and `last` is the later of that and the stamps of `caps` / `report`. The page is `stale` when it has been silent past its LEASE (`link_lease_s` 60 s for a visible page; `link_lease_hidden_s` 600 s for one that told the
-- seat it is hidden). `stale` means "nothing has been heard", not "gone".
Vm.peer_heard getter: prop(this.peer, 'heard')
Vm.last getter: max([x for x in [this.seen, this.peer_heard, this.heard] if x != None]) if len([x for x in [this.seen, this.peer_heard, this.heard] if x != None]) > 0 else None
Vm.hidden getter: prop(this.peer, 'hidden') if prop(this.peer, 'hidden') != None else this.was_hidden
Vm.lease_s getter: ([b.lk_lease_hidden for b in instances('Boot')] or [600])[0] if this.hidden == True else ([b.lk_lease for b in instances('Boot')] or [60])[0]
Vm.silent getter: clock - this.last if this.last != None else None
Vm.state getter:
  WHEN this.link == 'up': 'connected'
  ELSE WHEN this.link == 'gone': 'gone'
  ELSE WHEN this.last == None: None
  ELSE WHEN this.silent >= this.lease_s: 'stale'
  ELSE: 'connected'
-- a Vm with a `link` (written by the seat's peer driver, cliffvm:src/peers.rs: `up` or `gone`) is a PEER (a program on this machine over shared memory); any other is a page
Vm.kind getter: 'peer' if this.link != None else 'page'
-- WHO IS CONNECTED: `Peers:seat.up` lists the namespaces of the peers whose link is up. A page follows it by its link (`LinkAllow:log`), so a tab can come and go with its peer.
Peers.kind getter: 'peers'
Peers.up getter: [replace(v.eid, 'Vm:', '') for v in instances('Vm') if v.kind == 'peer' and v.link == 'up']
Boot.started on-setter peers-root:
  WHEN len([p for p in instances('Peers')]) == 0:
    ('Peers:seat').born = clock

Vm.caps on-setter link-caps:
  WHEN this.caps != None:
    this.connected_at = this.connected_at or clock
    this.seen = clock
Page.report on-setter link-report:
  WHEN this.report != None:
    ('Vm:' + this.ns).page = this
    ('Vm:' + this.ns).connected_at = prop('Vm:' + this.ns, 'connected_at') or clock
    ('Vm:' + this.ns).seen = clock

-- a restart: the roots of the last run are retired (their tabs report again only when they are loaded again)
Boot.old_vms getter: sql('SELECT k FROM c_field WHERE json_extract(body, \'$.cls\') = \'Vm\'') if this.started != None else None
Boot.old_vms on-setter link-sweep:
  WHEN this.old_vms != None:
    FOR r IN this.old_vms:
      (prop(r, 'k')).retire = True

-- A WRITE A PEER ASKED FOR (docs/graph-link.md, sections 7 and 18): a peer's `wr` over shared memory becomes `LinkWrite:<peer name>:<wid>` {peer, id, prop, value} (the seat's peer driver, cliffvm:src/peers.rs; the driver reads the verdict and retires it at once: a question is not memory),
-- and the verdict below decides. THE POLICY IS DATA, A FACT THE OWNER SETS: `peer_writes`, a list of rules `{peer, prefix}`: the peer whose namespace is `peer` may write the nodes whose id starts with `prefix`. No rule, no write: the default is
-- refusal, and the peer's receipt says which fact decides. The driver applies an accepted write as an ordinary outside write (writer `link:<peer>`); it never writes a node of another namespace, whatever the rules say.
Boot.pw_rules getter: this.peer_writes if this.peer_writes is List else []
LinkWrite.rules getter: ([b.pw_rules for b in instances('Boot')] or [[]])[0]
LinkWrite.allowed getter: len([r for r in this.rules if r['peer'] == this.peer and startswith(str(this.id), str(r['prefix']))]) > 0
LinkWrite.verdict getter: 'accept' if this.allowed else 'refused: no rule of the fact peer_writes lets ' + str(this.peer) + ' write ' + str(this.id)
